{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The lotteries have clear outcomes and probabilities. Lottery A offers 500 Euros with 100% probability, and Lottery B offers 1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability. The reason requires identifying a 'favourable outcome' and comparing the probabilities of achieving it, assuming outcome differences are negligible. Here, if we consider 'a favourable outcome' as any positive gain, both lotteries offer that (500 or 1000 Euros). However, since the reason treats outcome differences as negligible, 500 and 1000 Euros are considered similarly favourable. Thus, the relevant information is the probability of a favourable outcome: Lottery A has 100% probability (since 500 Euros is guaranteed), and Lottery B has 100% probability of a positive outcome (either 1000 or 0, but 1000 occurs 50% of the time). Wait — actually, Lottery B gives a positive outcome (1000) only 50% of the time, and 0 the other 50%. So the probability of a favourable (positive) outcome is 100% for A and 50% for B. Therefore, the reason would prefer Lottery A, as it has a higher probability of a favourable outcome.",
